course: french
lesson: 33
title: 14 juillet, poisson d'avril and other French days
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: France's year is dotted with special days, and each one has its own words, food or game.
explain: The biggest is France's national day, which English speakers often call Bastille Day but French people call le 14 Juillet or la fête nationale: a parade in Paris in the morning, fireworks at night and dances at the fire station.
explain: Picture 1 April in a French playground: children sneak up behind their friends and stick paper fish on their backs, then shout "Poisson d'avril !" - April fish, the French April fool.
explain: At Easter, French children are told that church bells fly away to Rome and come back on Easter Sunday, dropping chocolate eggs into gardens.
explain: Other days are quieter: on 1 May people give little white flowers for luck, and on 11 November whole villages stand in silence at the war memorial.
fact: France's national day is 14 July, le 14 Juillet or la fête nationale; English speakers often call it Bastille Day.
fact: It remembers 14 July 1789, when people in Paris took the Bastille, a prison fortress.
fact: On 14 July there is a big military parade (le défilé) on the Champs-Élysées, a famous avenue in Paris, planes trail blue, white and red smoke, and there are fireworks (le feu d'artifice) at night.
fact: Around 14 July many fire stations hold a dance open to everyone, le bal des pompiers; les pompiers are the firefighters.
fact: On 1 April French children stick paper fish on people's backs and shout "Poisson d'avril !" - the French April fool.
fact: In France church bells are silent before Easter; children are told les cloches de Pâques fly to Rome and bring back chocolate eggs on Easter Sunday.
fact: The Easter greeting is "Joyeuses Pâques !", with joyeuses, because Pâques is treated as feminine and plural in it.
fact: On 1 May people give little bunches of lily of the valley, le muguet, for good luck; 1 May is also a public holiday, la fête du Travail.
fact: On 21 June, la Fête de la musique, anyone can play music outside for free; it started in France and is now held in many countries.
fact: La rentrée is the return to school and work in early September, with new pencil cases and timetables.
fact: In France Mother's Day (la fête des Mères) is usually the last Sunday of May and Father's Day (la fête des Pères) the third Sunday of June; Mothering Sunday in the UK is in March.
fact: On 11 November people remember those who died in the First World War, which stopped on 11 November 1918; almost every town has a war memorial, le monument aux morts.
exercise: What do French people call their national day? | le 14 Juillet | le jour de la Bastille, only that | la fête du Travail | la rentrée nationale
exercise: What happened on 14 July 1789? | Parisians took the Bastille prison | The Eiffel Tower was opened to the public | The first Tour de France finished in Paris | France won its first football World Cup
exercise: What is le défilé on 14 July? | The parade | The fireworks | The picnic | The dance
exercise: Lily watches soldiers marching down a huge avenue in Paris. What is it called? | les Champs-Élysées | la tour Eiffel | le pont d'Avignon | la Bastille
exercise: Ella goes to a bal des pompiers. What is it? | A dance held by the firefighters | A football match against firefighters | A parade of fire engines | A lesson on putting out fires
exercise: What does "Poisson d'avril !" mean as a shout? | April fool! | Happy April! | Fish for dinner! | Spring is here!
exercise: What do French children stick on people's backs on 1 April? | Paper fish | Paper eggs | Paper hats | Real fish
exercise: Who brings the chocolate eggs at Easter in French stories? | The flying church bells | The big Easter bunny | Le Père Noël on his sledge | A big white hen
exercise: Aisha writes "Joyeux Pâques". What is right? | Joyeuses Pâques | Joyeux Pâque | Joyeuse Pâque | Bonne Pâques
exercise: What do people give on 1 May? | Bunches of muguet | Paper fish | Chocolate bells | Crêpes
exercise: What is la Fête de la musique? | Free street music on 21 June | A big concert in Paris on 14 July | A school singing contest in May | A music exam before la rentrée
exercise: What is la rentrée? | The return to school in September | The entrance to a building | The first day of the holidays | A big sale in the shops
exercise: When is Mother's Day usually in France? | The last Sunday of May | The second Sunday of March | The first Sunday of May | The third Sunday of June
exercise: What do people remember on 11 November? | The dead of the First World War | The day the Bastille was taken | The first day of winter | The birthday of the French king
exercise: What is le monument aux morts? | The war memorial | The town hall | The fire station | The church tower
exercise: What are les pompiers? | Firefighters | Police officers | Soldiers | Bakers
exercise: ★★★ How many years passed between the taking of the Bastille in 1789 and 14 July 2026? | 237 | 227 | 247 | 337
test: Jayden from London writes, "Happy Bastille Day, bruv!" and his penpal answers "Ici on dit le 14 Juillet !" What is she telling him? | In France they call it le 14 Juillet | Bastille Day is a different date | The French don't have a national day | It's rude to mention the Bastille
test: Lily from Ohio watches the parade and goes, "Who's Champs-Élysées? Is she, like, the queen?" What's the real answer? | It's an avenue in Paris | She's the queen of France | It's the name of a famous soldier | It's the name of the fireworks
test: Ella from Dundee says, "A firefighters' disco? Pure brilliant - but it's only for firemen, aye?" What's true? | Anyone can go: it's open to everyone | Only firefighters and their families | Only children under ten | Only people who live in Paris
test: Charlie from Liverpool says, "Boss prank, la - but why a fish?" (boss = great) What's true about the fish? | It's a paper fish: the French April fool | It's a real fish from the market | It's a fish-shaped cake for Easter | It's a prize for the best joke
test: Max from Bristol goes, "Flying bells instead of a bunny? Proper job, mate." What do French children hear? | The bells fly to Rome and bring eggs back | The bells ring to wake the Easter bunny | The bells are chocolate and you eat them | The bells fly to Paris for the parade
test: ★ Why is it "Joyeuses Pâques" with -euses, not "joyeux"? | In the greeting Pâques is feminine and plural | Easter is always a masculine word | It's a spelling mistake that everyone makes | -euses is used for every holiday
test: Sophie sees white flowers sold on every corner in Paris on 1 May and asks, "Is it someone's wedding?" What's going on? | People give muguet for good luck | It's the wedding day of the president | The flowers are for the war memorial | It's la rentrée, flowers for teachers
test: Josh from Lyon goes, "Is everyone busking? Mate, this is sick. Do I need a ticket?" (sick = great) What's the answer? | No, la Fête de la musique is free | Yes, every street needs a ticket | Yes, but only for children | No, because it's only on the radio
test: Grace from Belfast sees "La rentrée" in every shop in late August and asks, "Is that about going into something?" What does it mean? | Back to school after summer | The door you use to go in | The start of the summer holidays | A sale on Christmas presents
test: ★ Tom from Melbourne says, "Mother's Day is the same date everywhere, heaps easy." Is he right? | No, they differ: late May in France | Yes, every country has it in May | Yes, every country has it in March | No, France doesn't have one
test: ★★★ Kai from Auckland is in France on 11 November and asks, "Chur, how many years ago did the fighting stop, if it's 2026?" | 108 years | 118 years | 98 years | 88 years
test: What would happen if you talked loudly at the monument aux morts on 11 November? | People would find it rude | Everyone would join in the singing | You'd win a prize for the loudest voice | Nothing, it's a noisy party
exam: Identify the event commemorated in France on 14 July. | The taking of the Bastille in 1789 | The end of the First World War in 1918 | The opening of the Eiffel Tower in 1889 | The first crossing of the Channel by air
exam: Select the correct form of the French Easter greeting. | Joyeuses Pâques | Joyeux Pâques | Joyeuse Pâque | Joyeux Pâque
exam: Determine the date upon which the Fête de la musique is held. | 21 June | 14 July | 1 May | 11 November
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the term "la rentrée" as employed in France. | The return to school and work | The entrance of a public building | The commencement of the summer holidays | The annual festival of music
